      "title": "Somalia’s ex-spy chief does not rule out Al-Shabaab alliance to topple president - Somali Guardian",
      "link": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-ex-spy-chief-al-shabab-alliance-president/",
      "date": "Sun, 25 Oct 2026 18:23:54 GMT",
      "image": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/8.jpg",
      "content": "Somalia's ex-spy chief does not rule out Al-Shabaab alliance to topple president | Somali Guardian Facebook Instagram X VKontakte Youtube News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Search 27.2 C Mogadishu News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Privacy Policy Sign in Welcome! Log into your account your username your password Forgot your password? Get help Password recovery Recover your password your email A password will be e-mailed to you. Facebook Instagram X VKontakte Youtube Somali Guardian News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Home News Somalia Somalia’s ex-spy chief does not rule out Al-Shabaab alliance to topple president Somalia’s ex-spy chief does not rule out Al-Shabaab alliance to topple president October 25, 2026 Share Facebook X Pinterest WhatsApp MOGADISHU (Somaliguardian) – Somalia faces renewed fears of political instability after former intelligence chief Abdullahi Mohamed Ali Sanbalolshe said his Hawadle clan intends to establish a new regional administration in Hiran, raising the prospect of another confrontation with the federal government and intensifying concerns about the country’s fragile political order. In a recent interview with Gaylan Media, Sanbalolshe said the clan had decided to pursue a separate regional administration after being denied the opportunity to secure the presidency of Hirshabelle state under President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud. His remarks went beyond demands for political representation, suggesting that the proposed initiative could develop into an armed resistance movement against the government in Mogadishu. Sanbalolshe warned that Somalia was approaching a dangerous turning point, predicting a major war and arguing that the current political order was weakening. He said his clan wanted to secure an influential position in whatever political system emerged from the turmoil. The comments come amid separate warnings from Somali counterterrorism official Abdirahman Turyare, who recently alleged that plans were being developed to establish armed rebellions in Hiran and Middle Shabelle, potentially drawing the two regions into a wider confrontation with federal authorities. Sanbalolshe raises prospect of armed rebellion Sanbalolshe indicated that his movement would be prepared to take significant risks to remove Hassan Sheikh Mohamud’s government, suggesting that it would seek assistance from any party willing to support that objective. Asked whether his group would cooperate with Al-Shabaab, the Islamist militant organization fighting the Somali government, Sanbalolshe did not rule out the possibility. His remarks raise the prospect of a particularly volatile situation in which opponents of the federal government might consider tactical cooperation with a militant organization responsible for years of attacks against Somali security forces and officials. However, the interview account does not establish that Sanbalolshe or his associates have reached an agreement with Al-Shabaab, nor does it independently confirm that a coordinated armed campaign is underway. Sanbalolshe also said his movement would do worse than Southwest State leader Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Laftagareen, who has faced accusations of coordinating with Al-Shabaab during clashes with federal security forces in Baidoa and other major towns in Bay region. Sanbalolshe’s comparison nevertheless underscores the severity of the confrontation he appears to envisage if the dispute over regional power escalates into armed conflict. UAE meetings raise questions about regional involvement Sanbalolshe said he had recently travelled to the United Arab Emirates, where he met Somaliland President Abdirahman Mohamed Abdullahi, known as Irro; Puntland leader Said Abdullahi Deni; and Jubaland leader Ahmed Mohamed Islam, widely known as Ahmed Madobe. According to Sanbalolshe’s account, he urged the leaders to support an armed rebellion against the federal government in the Hiran region. It remains unclear whether any of the leaders agreed to his proposal or whether the reported meetings resulted in concrete political or military commitments. The reported outreach is significant because Somaliland, Puntland and Jubaland occupy distinct positions in Somalia’s contested political landscape. Any involvement by their leaders in a campaign against the federal government could deepen existing divisions between Mogadishu and regional authorities. Sanbalolshe also said the proposed movement would seek assistance from neighbouring Ethiopia, potentially extending the dispute beyond Somalia’s domestic political arena. The reported discussions and proposed external support have not been independently verified. No conclusion can be drawn from Sanbalolshe’s statements alone about the involvement of foreign governments or regional leaders in plans for an armed rebellion. Nevertheless, the prospect of outside support for a new anti-government movement could complicate efforts to contain tensions and maintain cooperation among Somalia’s political and security institutions. Counterterrorism official warns of two planned rebellions Sanbalolshe’s comments come just days after Somali counterterrorism official Abdirahman Turyare warned that plans were being developed to establish two armed rebellions in Hiran and Middle Shabelle. Turyare alleged that the proposed movements were being financed by the United Arab Emirates and intended to cooperate with Al-Shabaab in a campaign against the federal government. He urged the authorities to intervene as early as possible to prevent the alleged plans from materialising. The timing of the two sets of remarks is likely to intensify scrutiny of the security situation in central Somalia. It is also unclear whether the alleged rebellions described by Turyare are directly connected to the initiative outlined by Sanbalolshe, or whether the two statements refer to the same organized effort. Contact us: info@somaliguardian.com Share Facebook X Pinterest WhatsApp Previous article Ethiopia-style guerrilla insurgencies could emerge in Somalia: official Somaliguardian news RELATED ARTICLES MORE FROM AUTHOR Somalia Ethiopia-style guerrilla insurgencies could emerge in Somalia: official Somalia Somalia sends more than 200 military vehicles toward Burhakabo Somalia Somalia opposition scores own goal over Galmudug election Somalia Somalia: Key Laftagaren ally survives alleged Turkish drone strike Somalia Somalia’s opposition launches first court challenge to new constitution Somalia Somalia parliament speaker accuses Kenya of harbouring terrorists Somalia Somalia angry as Ethiopia refuses to intervene in South West State conflict Somalia Somalia police enter Hormuud headquarters in Mogadishu amid Southwest state tensions Somalia Somalia president rejects opposition calls for consensus-based election Somalia Somalia’s government loses all major Bay towns but Baidoa to Laftagaren Somalia Somalia’s government loses Burhakabo to ousted Southwest leader’s forces Somalia Somalia agrees to buy Pakistani JF-17 fighters ABOUT US Somaliguardian is an East African leading news website. We are impartial and independent, every day we create distinctive, world class content which inform and educate thousands of people in Somalia, East Africa and around the world. Get updated with our real time news coverage from Somalia, Africa and the entire world. FOLLOW US Facebook Instagram X VKontakte Youtube News Somalia Africa World Featured Editor Picks Editorials Privacy Policy © 2021 Somaliguardian error: Content is protected!",
      "summary": "Somalia's political landscape is facing renewed instability as former intelligence chief Abdullahi Mohamed Ali Sanbalolshe announced his Hawadle clan's intention to establish a new regional administration in Hiran. This move follows their dissatisfaction with the federal government's handling of political representation, particularly regarding the presidency of Hirshabelle state. Sanbalolshe's comments suggest a potential shift towards armed resistance against President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud's government, raising fears of a major conflict. He did not dismiss the possibility of collaborating with Al-Shabaab, the militant group opposing the government, which could lead to a dangerous alliance. Additionally, Sanbalolshe has sought support from regional leaders in the UAE for an armed rebellion, although it remains unclear if any agreements were reached. Concurrently, Somali counterterrorism officials have warned of planned rebellions in Hiran and Middle Shabelle, allegedly financed by the UAE, further complicating the already fragile political situation in Somalia.",
